---
// src/components/OtherIslandSection.astro
//
// Closing full-bleed illustration for the bottom of the page — not
// part of the flanking-islands family (ArtIslandSection /
// ComputerIslandSection / EducationIslandSection /
// AchievementIslandSection). No text, no mouse-repel, no bob loop —
// just one image that always spans the full viewport width, edge to
// edge, regardless of screen size.
//
// "Full viewport width no matter what" is done with the standard
// break-out-of-the-container trick:
//   width: 100vw; position: relative; left: 50%; right: 50%;
//   margin-left: -50vw; margin-right: -50vw;
// `width: 100%` alone would only match the width of whatever contains
// it. Right now nothing in Layout.astro constrains that (it's a bare
// `<slot />` in `<body>`), so `100%` would likely look identical today
// — but the 100vw break-out doesn't depend on that staying true, so
// this keeps working even if a max-width wrapper gets added around
// the page later. `overflow-x: hidden` on the section guards against
// the one common side effect of this trick: 100vw includes the
// vertical scrollbar's width, which can introduce a few px of
// horizontal overflow on some browsers.
//
// Height is unconstrained on every breakpoint (`height: auto`), so
// the image always keeps its own natural aspect ratio and the WHOLE
// illustration shows, edge to edge, with no cropping and no gap down
// either side — desktop's `image` is a wide/landscape piece, so this
// looks right there without any special handling.
//
// Mobile went through several different approaches before landing
// back on this same plain `height: auto` behavior — worth recording
// why, so a future "make mobile look taller/shorter/etc." request
// doesn't just re-walk the same dead ends:
//   1. A `max-width: 640px` block gave the section a real `height:
//      100vh` box and switched the image to `object-fit: cover` so it
//      filled that box by CROPPING instead of flattening out (this
//      was written back when mobile still showed the same wide
//      `image` as desktop, which really did look too short/flattened
//      at narrow widths — seed history, not current behavior).
//   2. Per your request, `100vh` (which varies with each PHONE's own
//      screen height) was swapped for a flat `700px`, so the section
//      was the same height on every device regardless of that
//      device's own viewport height.
//   3. Once `mobileImage` (`other_island.webp`, a tall PORTRAIT piece
//      painted specifically for phones — see the "New:" note below)
//      replaced the old shared `image` on mobile, `cover` inside that
//      `700px` box started cropping the BOTTOM off of it on wider
//      mobile widths — so `object-fit: contain` replaced `cover`,
//      trading the vertical crop for empty space beside or below the
//      image instead (whichever dimension had room left over).
//   4. Per your follow-up, that leftover space showed up as visible
//      gaps down the LEFT/RIGHT sides on wider mobile widths, which
//      you didn't want either — and that's the point where the fixed-
//      height box itself had to go: a box that's simultaneously (a)
//      always the exact same height AND (b) always exactly as wide as
//      the viewport AND (c) never crops any of a portrait image is not
//      achievable at the same time — (a) and (b) together pin the
//      box's own aspect ratio, and only ONE image aspect ratio could
//      ever fill that exact box with zero cropping and zero leftover
//      space, which isn't true across a whole range of phone widths.
//      Dropping the fixed height (back to plain `height: auto`, same
//      as desktop) is what actually satisfies "no cropping" AND "no
//      side gaps" AT ONCE — the trade-off is that the section's height
//      now scales with the viewport's WIDTH (taller on a wider phone),
//      same as it always has for desktop's `image`, rather than being
//      a hardcoded constant. No more `max-width: 640px` block at all
//      below — mobile and desktop now share the exact same sizing
//      rules; only the source FILE differs between them (via
//      `mobileImage`/`<picture>`, see below).
//
// No `object-fit`/`object-position` anywhere any more (see the
// numbered history above) — with plain `height: auto` on every
// breakpoint, the image is never fit into a box that's a different
// shape than itself in the first place, so there's nothing to crop or
// position. `mobileImage` (`other_island.webp`, a tall PORTRAIT piece
// — 3840x6320 — painted specifically for phones, see the "New:
// optional `mobileImage` prop" note above) simply renders at whatever
// height its own aspect ratio produces at the current viewport width,
// same as desktop's `image` always has.
//
// No seam-fade classes any more — the shared fade-band system that
// used to blend section edges together was removed per your request,
// so this section now just butts directly against BonusIslandSection.
//
// Now also carries `.halftone-hover`, same as the rest of the chain —
// joins the single, page-wide cursor-spotlight element in
// Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`) purely by having this class,
// which is all `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover) .halftone-spotlight`
// needs to turn the spotlight on while the cursor's over this
// section. No per-section markup or script needed for it any more —
// an earlier version added a `.halftone-overlay` div + script to
// every section individually, but that ran into exactly this
// section's own `overflow-x: hidden` (needed for the 100vw break-out
// above) computing its `overflow-y` to `auto` per the CSS overflow
// spec, which clipped that per-section overlay's bleed and cut the
// spotlight off at this section's top edge. The page-wide version
// doesn't have a per-section box to clip in the first place, so this
// section needs nothing beyond the `.halftone-hover` class itself.
//
// File size: you've since swapped the original ~45MB base64-in-SVG
// export for other_island_2.webp (~2.4MB) — a big improvement, though
// still worth a look if you want to shave it down further (a well
// compressed webp of a similar sky/landscape scene can often get well
// under 1MB with no visible quality loss). `loading="lazy"` is still
// on the <img> below so it doesn't compete with anything above it for
// bandwidth on initial load either way.
//
// New: optional `mobileImage` prop — per your request, on screens
// `640px` and narrower (the same breakpoint the `.other-section`
// mobile crop above already uses) the section now renders
// `other_island.webp`, an illustration you made specifically for
// mobile, instead of the desktop `other_island_2.webp`. This is done
// with a `<picture>` + `<source media="...">` (see the template
// below) rather than a CSS media query on a `background-image`, since
// the section renders an actual `<img>` element, not a background —
// `<picture>` is the standard, JS-free way to swap which image FILE
// loads per breakpoint (a CSS approach could only swap how an
// already-downloaded image is cropped/positioned, not which file the
// browser fetches in the first place, which matters for mobile data
// usage too: phones never even download `other_island_2.webp`). Both
// images still go through the exact same `.other-section-image`
// class and the `max-width: 640px` crop rules below, so this is
// purely about which file loads, not how it's displayed once loaded.
// `mobileImage` is optional — omit it and mobile just keeps using the
// same `image` as every other breakpoint, same as before this prop
// existed.
//
// Usage:
//   <OtherIslandSection
//     id="section-closing"
//     image="/images/other_island_2.webp"
//     mobileImage="/images/other_island.webp"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  image: string;
  /** Optional mobile-specific replacement for `image`, used instead of
   *  it on screens `640px` and narrower (see the "New:" note above).
   *  Omit it and mobile just renders `image` like every other
   *  breakpoint. */
  mobileImage?: string;
  alt?: string;
}

const { id, image, mobileImage, alt = "" } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="other-section sky-chain halftone-hover" id={id}>
  <picture>
    {mobileImage && <source media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={mobileImage} />}
    <img class="other-section-image" src={image} alt={alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  </picture>
</section>

<style>
  .other-section {
    position: relative;
    width: 100vw;
    left: 50%;
    right: 50%;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    margin-right: -50vw;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .other-section-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* New: no `max-width: 640px` block here any more — mobile used to
     get its own `height: 100vh`, then a flat `700px`, box with
     `object-fit: cover`/`contain` on the image inside it; see the
     numbered history in the component-level comment above for why
     all of that was removed. Mobile and desktop now share the exact
     same two rules above (`width: 100%; height: auto;`) — only the
     source FILE differs between them, via `mobileImage`/`<picture>`. */
</style>